; Peer review report / Calibration note; AI-use disclosure / Prompt audit trail", "High-value Verbs to Add / Emphasize": "Iterate, critique, render, compose, curate, revise, explain choices, adapt to constraints", "Why this fit is strong": "Creative work is best assessed through artifacts, process, critique, criteria, reflection, and revision.", "AI-resistant design moves": "Require process book, critique response, version history, audience/client constraints, and live presentation.", "Primary Source Keys": "Design_Critique_2025, Tai_2018, Jonsson_Svingby_2007", "Source URLs": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s44217-025-00824-9; https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10734-017-0220-3; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ796733"}, {"Discipline / Program Cluster": "Public policy / Public health", "Particularly Appropriate Task Types": "Scenario; Real world project; Case-based decision memo; Structured debate / Moot court; Data analysis practicum; Simulation game / Business game; Community-based project; Policy lab / Stakeholder briefing; AI audit / Source verification task", "Recommended Deliverables": "Policy brief / Stakeholder brief; Data visualization / Dashboard; Decision memo / Recommendation brief; Source analysis packet / Evidence matrix; Presentation; Portfolio reflection / Self-assessment; AI-use disclosure / Prompt audit trail", "High-value Verbs to Add / Emphasize": "Synthesize, advocate, weigh, mitigate, forecast, communicate for audience, evaluate equity", "Why this fit is strong": "Work requires evidence synthesis, feasibility judgment, stakeholder communication, ethics/equity, and actionable recommendations.", "AI-resistant design moves": "Use local data, stakeholder constraints, briefings with Q&A, evidence matrices, and transparent tool/source use.", "Primary Source Keys": "Villarroel_2018, Gulikers_2004, Vlachopoulos_Makri_2024, GenAI_Assessment_2024", "Source URLs": "https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ1181322; https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02504676; https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0191491X24001044; https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s41239-024-00468-z"}, {"Discipline / Program Cluster": "Criminal justice / Legal studies", "Particularly Appropriate Task Types": "Simulation; Scenario; Role Play; Case-based decision memo; Structured debate / Moot court; Primary-source/document-based assessment; AI audit / Source verification task", "Recommended Deliverables": "Debate brief / Argument map; Source analysis packet / Evidence matrix; Decision memo / Recommendation brief; Client communication artifact; Oral defense / Viva response; Report", "High-value Verbs to Add / Emphasize": "Argue, defend, corroborate, weigh, refute, comply, apply procedure, evaluate evidence", "Why this fit is strong": "Legal/criminal justice learning emphasizes evidence, procedure, argument, ethics, and judgment under constraints.", "AI-resistant design moves": "Use assigned fact patterns, changing facts, oral argument, procedural criteria, evidence matrices, and response to questioning.", "Primary Source Keys": "Argument_Rubrics_2013, Kane_2013, Wineburg_1991", "Source URLs": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1068572.pdf; https://www.ets.org/research/policy_research_reports/publications/article/201...; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ436869"}, {"Discipline / Program Cluster": "Library / Information / Media literacy", "Particularly Appropriate Task Types": "Scenario; Inquiry / Investigation; Primary-source/document-based assessment; Peer review / Calibration; Process-product portfolio assessment; AI audit / Source verification task", "Recommended Deliverables": "Source analysis packet / Evidence matrix; AI-use disclosure / Prompt audit trail; Report; Peer review report / Calibration note; Oral defense / Viva response; Portfolio reflection / Self-assessment", "High-value Verbs to Add / Emphasize": "Verify, source, search, triangulate, annotate, evaluate credibility, disclose tool use", "Why this fit is strong": "Information literacy assessment focuses on source evaluation, search strategy, evidence credibility, and transparent tool use.", "AI-resistant design moves": "Use assigned/local source sets, audit trails, source verification tasks, and oral explanation of search decisions.", "Primary Source Keys": "Smith_Breakstone_Wineburg_2019, Alkouk_Khlaif_2024, GenAI_Assessment_2024", "Source URLs": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/pdf/48541494.pdf; https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.14...; https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s41239-024-00468-z"}, {"Discipline / Program Cluster": "Exercise science / Kinesiology / Athletic training", "Particularly Appropriate Task Types": "Real world demonstration; Performance task / Skills check; OSCE / Standardized client station; Fieldwork / Practicum evidence; Lab practicum; Case-based decision memo; Process-product portfolio assessment; Community-based project", "Recommended Deliverables": "Rubric-scored observation checklist; Clinical note / Care plan / Handoff; Portfolio reflection / Self-assessment; Lab notebook / Research log; Process log / Design notebook; Report", "High-value Verbs to Add / Emphasize": "Assess movement, cue, prescribe, monitor, adapt, coach, document, evaluate risk", "Why this fit is strong": "Exercise science outcomes often require observable practical technique, client interaction, safety, interpretation of physiological data, and professional judgment.", "AI-resistant design moves": "Use observed skills checks, client-specific data, practicum evidence, supervisor feedback, and reflection on coaching/technique choices.", "Primary Source Keys": "Exercise_WIL_2010, Miller_1990, OSCE_Validity_2018, Hofstein_Lunetta_2004", "Source URLs": "https://www.ijwil.org/files/APJCE_11_3_153_167.pdf; https://academic.oup.com/academicmedicine/article-abstract/65/9/S63/8361091; https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12909-018-1421-x; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ759842"}, {"Discipline / Program Cluster": "Sociology / Gender studies / Interdisciplinary social inquiry", "Particularly Appropriate Task Types": "Scenario; Inquiry / Investigation; Community-based project; Primary-source/document-based assessment; Structured debate / Moot court; Process-product portfolio assessment; Peer review / Calibration; Data analysis practicum; AI audit / Source verification task", "Recommended Deliverables": "Source analysis packet / Evidence matrix; Report; Policy brief / Stakeholder brief; Debate brief / Argument map; Portfolio reflection / Self-assessment; Peer review report / Calibration note; Data visualization / Dashboard", "High-value Verbs to Add / Emphasize": "Analyze power, compare perspectives, code data, evaluate positionality, synthesize, critique assumptions", "Why this fit is strong": "These fields often assess theory application, qualitative/quantitative evidence, reflexivity, structural analysis, and communication about contested issues.", "AI-resistant design moves": "Use local/community data, positionality/reflexive process notes, assigned source sets, peer calibration, and transparent source/AI-use trails.", "Primary Source Keys": "FeministReflective_2025, Argument_Rubrics_2013, Villarroel_2018, Tai_2018", "Source URLs": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09540253.2025.2508761; https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1068572.pdf; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ1181322; https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10734-017-0220-3"}];

  var AI_RESISTANCE = [{"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Process-product evidence", "Why it helps validity/security": "Assesses both final product and how the learner arrived there.", "Works best for": "Projects, writing, design, coding, research", "Example implementation": "Require draft history, feedback use, revision memo, and final artifact.", "Caution": "Do not overvalue busywork; define representative evidence.", "Source Keys": "Alkouk_Khlaif_2024; Boud_Falchikov_2006; Eportfolio_2024", "Source URLs": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1499495/full; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ736100; https://journals.librarypublishing.arizona.edu/itlt/article/id/5809/"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Oral defense / follow-up questions", "Why it helps validity/security": "Probes reasoning, assumptions, and transfer beyond a submitted artifact.", "Works best for": "High-stakes work, projects, models, code, writing", "Example implementation": "Two to five structured questions after submission.", "Caution": "Needs rubrics/common prompts to maintain fairness.", "Source Keys": "Kane_2013; Alkouk_Khlaif_2024", "Source URLs": "https://www.ets.org/research/policy_research_reports/publications/article/2013/jpdc.html; https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1499495/full"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Observed performance", "Why it helps validity/security": "Provides direct evidence of skills that cannot be represented fully in text.", "Works best for": "Clinical, lab, teaching, performance, technical skills", "Example implementation": "Video/in-person demonstration scored with criteria.", "Caution": "Privacy/accessibility accommodations may be needed.", "Source Keys": "Miller_1990; OSCE_Validity_2018; Jonsson_Svingby_2007", "Source URLs": "https://academic.oup.com/academicmedicine/article-abstract/65/9/S63/8361091; https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12909-018-1421-x; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ796733"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Local or learner-generated data", "Why it helps validity/security": "Constrains task to evidence the learner gathered or a local context AI cannot infer.", "Works best for": "Labs, fieldwork, statistics, community projects", "Example implementation": "Use campus/community observations, unique datasets, field notes.", "Caution": "Protect privacy and avoid burdensome access requirements.", "Source Keys": "Gulikers_2004; Hofstein_Lunetta_2004; Villarroel_2018", "Source URLs": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02504676; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ759842; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ1181322"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Dynamic scenarios", "Why it helps validity/security": "Changing information requires situated decision-making rather than static text generation.", "Works best for": "Simulations, cases, troubleshooting, clinical judgment", "Example implementation": "Release case updates and ask learners to revise decisions.", "Caution": "Avoid unnecessary complexity; align updates to outcomes.", "Source Keys": "Issenberg_2005; Gosen_Washbush_2004", "Source URLs": "https://www.unboundmedicine.com/medline/citation/16147767/Features_and_uses_of_high_fidelity_medical_simulations_that_lead_to_effective_learning%3A_a_BEME_systematic_review_; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ962209"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Criteria-based peer/self evaluation", "Why it helps validity/security": "Develops evaluative judgment and provides additional evidence of criteria use.", "Works best for": "Writing, design, projects, presentations", "Example implementation": "Peer review with calibration against exemplars.", "Caution": "Train students on criteria; do not rely solely on peer grades.", "Source Keys": "Tai_2018; Dochy_1999; Jonsson_Svingby_2007", "Source URLs": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10734-017-0220-3; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ609990; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ796733"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Authentic audience/stakeholder", "Why it helps validity/security": "Forces adaptation of communication, evidence, and recommendations to context.", "Works best for": "Policy, business, health, education, communication", "Example implementation": "Briefing for a role-specific stakeholder with Q&A.", "Caution": "Avoid simulated authenticity that adds irrelevant barriers.", "Source Keys": "Gulikers_2004; Villarroel_2018", "Source URLs": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02504676; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ1181322"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "AI-use disclosure and verification trail", "Why it helps validity/security": "Assesses responsible tool use instead of pretending AI was not available.", "Works best for": "Courses allowing AI/digital tools", "Example implementation": "Prompt log, AI output, verification sources, human edits, decision rationale.", "Caution": "Only use when policy permits AI; be clear about allowed/prohibited uses.", "Source Keys": "Alkouk_Khlaif_2024; GenAI_Assessment_2024", "Source URLs": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1499495/full; https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s41239-024-00468-z"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Rubrics with exemplars/rater training", "Why it helps validity/security": "Improves consistency when assessing complex performance evidence.", "Works best for": "Any performance assessment, especially high-stakes", "Example implementation": "Analytic rubric, anchor examples, norming discussion.", "Caution": "Rubrics do not guarantee validity by themselves; align to outcome.", "Source Keys": "Jonsson_Svingby_2007; Panadero_Jonsson_2013; Kane_2013", "Source URLs": "https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ796733; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ999454; https://www.ets.org/research/policy_research_reports/publications/article/2013/jpdc.html"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Individual accountability within teams", "Why it helps validity/security": "Separates collaboration evidence from individual learning evidence.", "Works best for": "Team projects, simulations, capstones", "Example implementation": "Contribution statements, peer evaluation, individual oral questions.", "Caution": "Avoid punitive peer ratings; use multiple evidence sources.", "Source Keys": "Chi_Wylie_2014; Dochy_1999; Vlachopoulos_Makri_2024", "Source URLs": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1044018.pdf; https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ609990; https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0191491X24001044"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Hyper-Local / Context-Specific Grounding", "Why it helps validity/security": "Forces the assessment to rely on highly recent, proprietary, or geographically constrained parameters that bypass general LLM training data or pre-programmed knowledge cutoffs.", "Works best for": "Reports, Policy Briefs, Case Studies, Social Sciences, Educational Leadership", "Example implementation": "Analyzing a niche community dispute or a brand-new local school board policy using firsthand stakeholder interviews or local meeting notes.", "Caution": "Can introduce equity disparities if some students lack equal physical access to local sites or niche resources.", "Source Keys": "Faculty_Focus_2026", "Source URLs": "https://www.facultyfocus.com/articles/educational-assessment/designing-ai-resistant-assignments-in-educational-leadership-courses/"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Process-Based Tracking (Process-Product Assessment Model)", "Why it helps validity/security": "Shifts evaluation from the final polished document to the chronological evolution of the work, rendering \"one-shot\" AI text generation completely visible and inadequate.", "Works best for": "Computer Science (Code Repositories), Long-form Essays, Capstones, Research Plans", "Example implementation": "Requiring students to submit interactive version-tracked drafts (e.g., git commit history logs) detailing explicit revisions made in response to milestones.", "Caution": "Markedly increases the grading and tracking workload for instructors monitoring multiple touchpoints.", "Source Keys": "Frontiers_2024;eSchool_News_2026", "Source URLs": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1499495/full;https://www.eschoolnews.com/digital-learning/2026/03/12/ai-classrooms-designing-assessment/"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Live Interactive Defense (Viva Voce)", "Why it helps validity/security": "Validates genuine intellectual ownership and conversational agility through unscripted, spontaneous verbal explanation, instantly peeling away superficial AI fluency.", "Works best for": "Presentations, Group Projects, Business Pitches, Senior Capstones", "Example implementation": "Implementing a brief 3-to-5 minute synchronous Q&A session post-submission where students must verbally defend specific technical design choices.", "Caution": "Highly time-prohibitive in large classes and can inadvertently penalize non-native speakers or induce severe anxiety.", "Source Keys": "eSchool_News_2026; FeedbackFruits_2026", "Source URLs": "https://www.eschoolnews.com/digital-learning/2026/03/12/ai-classrooms-designing-assessment/;https://feedbackfruits.com/blog/ai-resilient-assessment-design-higher-education"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "AI Output Auditing & Multi-Layered Annotation", "Why it helps validity/security": "Moves higher-order evaluation to the forefront by framing a flawed AI-generated artifact as the direct object of critique, targeting student discernment instead of text production.", "Works best for": "Code Reviews, Fact-Checking Exercises, Literature Summaries, Answer Questions", "Example implementation": "Supplying students with a highly fluent but factually hollow AI synthesis of a reading and requiring them to cross-verify and annotate its omissions.", "Caution": "Demands a high level of baseline AI and data literacy from both the students and the instructional staff to grade fairly.", "Source Keys": "Grand_Valley_2026", "Source URLs": "https://services.gvsu.edu/TDClient/60/Portal/KB/PrintArticle?ID=28598"}, {"AI-resistant Design Feature": "Empirical / Fieldwork Data Integration", "Why it helps validity/security": "Anchors the conceptual and analytical output to un-scrapable, student-generated raw datasets collected in a specific time and environment.", "Works best for": "Lab Reports, Physical Science Experiments, Observational Logs", "Example implementation": "Building a quantitative analysis model entirely dependent on unique data parameters measured by the student during a live lab or site visit.", "Caution": "Highly dependent on successful data collection; if a student's data collection fails, the entire assessment pipeline breaks down.", "Source Keys": "Faculty_Focus_2026", "Source URLs": "https://www.facultyfocus.com/articles/educational-assessment/designing-ai-resistant-assignments-in-educational-leadership-courses/"}];

  var SOURCES = [{"Source Key": "Kane_2013", "Full Reference": "Kane, M. T. (2013). Validating the interpretations and uses of test scores. Journal of Educational Measurement, 50(1), 1-73.", "Assessment Implication": "Argument-based validity; score uses require evidence for claims, assumptions, and consequences.", "URL": "https://www.ets.org/research/policy_research_reports/publications/article/2013/jpdc.html"}, {"Source Key": "Biggs_1996", "Full Reference": "Biggs, J. (1996). Enhancing teaching through constructive alignment. Higher Education, 32, 347-364.", "Assessment Implication": "Learning outcomes should nominate performances of understanding that are aligned with teaching and assessment.", "URL": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00138871"}, {"Source Key": "Gulikers_2004", "Full Reference": "Gulikers, J. T. M., Bastiaens, T. J., & Kirschner, P. A. (2004). A five-dimensional framework for authentic assessment. Educational Technology Research and Development, 52(3), 67-86.", "Assessment Implication": "Authentic assessment can be designed across task, physical context, social context, result/form, and criteria.", "URL": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02504676"}, {"Source Key": "Villarroel_2018", "Full Reference": "Villarroel, V., Bloxham, S., Bruna, D., Bruna, C., & Herrera-Seda, C. (2018). Authentic assessment: creating a blueprint for course design. Assessment & Evaluation in Higher Education, 43(5), 840-854.", "Assessment Implication": "Authentic assessment replicates tasks and performance standards in real/professional contexts and supports employability-related learning.", "URL": "https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ1181322"}, {"Source Key": "Boud_Falchikov_2006", "Full Reference": "Boud, D., & Falchikov, N. (2006). Aligning assessment with long-term learning. Assessment & Evaluation in Higher Education, 31(4), 399-413.", "Assessment Implication": "Assessment should prepare learners for future practice and position students as assessors of quality.", "URL": "https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ736100"}, {"Source Key": "Tai_2018", "Full Reference": "Tai, J., Ajjawi, R., Boud, D., Dawson, P., & Panadero, E. (2018). Developing evaluative judgement: enabling students to make decisions about quality of work. Higher Education, 76, 467-481.", "Assessment Implication": "Evaluative judgment is the capability to judge the quality of one's own and others' work.", "URL": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10734-017-0220-3"}, {"Source Key": "Jonsson_Svingby_2007", "Full Reference": "Jonsson, A., & Svingby, G. (2007). The use of scoring rubrics: Reliability, validity and educational consequences. Educational Research Review, 2(2), 130-144.", "Assessment Implication": "Analytic, topic-specific rubrics with exemplars and rater training can improve reliability of performance scoring.", "URL": "https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ796733"}, {"Source Key": "Panadero_Jonsson_2013", "Full Reference": "Panadero, E., & Jonsson, A. (2013). The use of scoring rubrics for formative assessment purposes revisited: A review. Educational Research Review, 9, 129-144.", "Assessment Implication": "Rubrics can support formative assessment, self-assessment, peer assessment, and self-regulation when used actively.", "URL": "https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ999454"}, {"Source Key": "Dochy_1999", "Full Reference": "Dochy, F., Segers, M., & Sluijsmans, D. (1999). The use of self-, peer and co-assessment in higher education: A review. Studies in Higher Education, 24(3), 331-350.", "Assessment Implication": "Self-, peer-, and co-assessment can support reflection, responsibility, and professional competencies.", "URL": "https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ609990"}, {"Source Key": "Chi_Wylie_2014", "Full Reference": "Chi, M. T. H., & Wylie, R. (2014). The ICAP framework: Linking cognitive engagement to active learning outcomes. Educational Psychologist, 49(4), 219-243.", "Assessment Implication": "Observable engagement can be passive, active, constructive, or interactive; constructive/interactive tasks elicit deeper processing.", "URL": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1044018.pdf"}, {"Source Key": "Vlachopoulos_Makri_2024", "Full Reference": "Vlachopoulos, D., & Makri, A. (2024). A systematic literature review on authentic assessment in higher education. Studies in Educational Evaluation, 83, 101425.", "Assessment Implication": "Authentic assessment uses real-world tasks to evaluate knowledge, skills, and attitudes and can develop 21st-century skills.", "URL": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0191491X24001044"}, {"Source Key": "Alkouk_Khlaif_2024", "Full Reference": "Alkouk, W. A., & Khlaif, Z. N. (2024). AI-resistant assessments in higher education: practical insights from faculty training workshops. Frontiers in Education, 9.", "Assessment Implication": "AI-resistant assessment can emphasize process-product evidence, critical thinking, creativity, collaboration, and transparent AI use.", "URL": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1499495/full"}, {"Source Key": "GenAI_Assessment_2024", "Full Reference": "Liu et al. (2024). A scoping review on how generative artificial intelligence transforms assessment in higher education. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education.", "Assessment Implication": "Generative AI creates assessment-integrity challenges and opportunities; assessment designs should reconsider tasks, evidence, and use of AI.", "URL": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s41239-024-00468-z"}, {"Source Key": "Miller_1990", "Full Reference": "Miller, G. E. (1990). The assessment of clinical skills/competence/performance. Academic Medicine, 65(9), S63-S67.", "Assessment Implication": "Competence assessment can move from knowledge to knows-how, shows-how, and does, supporting observed performance tasks.", "URL": "https://academic.oup.com/academicmedicine/article-abstract/65/9/S63/8361091"}, {"Source Key": "Issenberg_2005", "Full Reference": "Issenberg, S. B., McGaghie, W. C., Petrusa, E. R., Gordon, D. L., & Scalese, R. J. (2005). Features and uses of high-fidelity medical simulations that lead to effective learning. Medical Teacher, 27(1), 10-28.", "Assessment Implication": "Simulation is effective when it provides feedback, repeated practice, integration, graded difficulty, and defined outcomes.", "URL": "https://www.unboundmedicine.com/medline/citation/16147767/Features_and_uses_of_high_fidelity_medical_simulations_that_lead_to_effective_learning%3A_a_BEME_systematic_review_"}, {"Source Key": "OSCE_Validity_2018", "Full Reference": "Pierre et al. / BMC Medical Education (2018). Evaluating the validity evidence of an OSCE: results from a new medical school.", "Assessment Implication": "OSCEs require validity evidence and can assess clinical performance using structured stations and scoring.", "URL": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12909-018-1421-x"}, {"Source Key": "Hofstein_Lunetta_2004", "Full Reference": "Hofstein, A., & Lunetta, V. N. (2004). The laboratory in science education: Foundations for the twenty-first century. Science Education, 88(1), 28-54.", "Assessment Implication": "Laboratory assessment can elicit inquiry, evidence-based explanation, and authentic scientific practice.", "URL": "https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ759842"}, {"Source Key": "DarlingHammond_Snyder_2000", "Full Reference": "Darling-Hammond, L., & Snyder, J. (2000). Authentic assessment of teaching in context. Teaching and Teacher Education, 16(5-6), 523-545.", "Assessment Implication": "Cases, exhibitions, portfolios, and action research can validly assess teaching in context.", "URL": "https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ610676"}, {"Source Key": "Gosen_Washbush_2004", "Full Reference": "Gosen, J., & Washbush, J. (2004). A review of scholarship on assessing experiential learning effectiveness. Simulation & Gaming, 35(2), 270-293.", "Assessment Implication": "Business games/simulations and experiential approaches need car